^Possessive suffixes and ownership^
In English you would say ‘my cup’ when you refer to a specific cup that belongs to you. In Finnish you do it with a suffix, *-ni*, *kuppini*. For most words you also use the genitive form before you add the suffix, e.g. *puhelin, puhelimen* becomes *puhelimeni* (my phone) and *Suomi, Suomen* becomes *Suomeni* (my Finland), but there are many more rules for the stem changes with some words. 

Some exceptions:
jalka, jalan --> jalkani
käsi, käden --> käteni
huone, huoneen --> huoneeni

The other pronouns each have their own possessive suffixes:

English	           Finnish	                                   Example
my		                    +ni		                               otan kirjani (I take my book)
your		                  +si		                               otat kirjasi (you take your book)
his/her		             +nsa		                            hän ottaa kirjansa (he takes his book)
our		                   +mme		                          otamme kirjamme (we take our book)
your		                 +nne		                             otatte kirjanne (you take your book)
their		                 +nsa		                             he ottavat kirjansa (they take their book)

Note: the words with possessive suffixes do not differ from their plural counterparts, so *jalkani* can mean both ‘my leg’ and ‘my legs’. The difference is known from context.

These suffixes can also be added after the *-ssa/-sta/-een* suffixes we saw earlier:

jalassani - 'in my leg'
kirjastasi - 'from your book'
huoneeseemme - 'into our room'